\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Mean-field mechanism versus empirics. (a)\nobreakspace  {}Mechanism (schematic): the cooperative gradient signal grows with centralisation ($\propto w\,g$) while partner exploration injects a roughly $w$-independent noise floor; $w_{\mathrm  {crit}}$ is the signal-to-noise crossing \textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:snr}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}, below which the lazy policy is the effective attractor. (b)\nobreakspace  {}The \emph  {derived affine} loss-aversion law \textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:wcrit_lambda}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}: CPT predicts $\phi (\lambda )=1+\rho (\lambda -1)$ with slope $\rho \in [0,1]$; the single data-implied slope $\rho =0.61$ respects the bound and the law tracks the measured $w_{\mathrm  {crit}}$ (RMSE $0.013$), the $\lambda {=}7$ point being the lone concave residual. (c)\nobreakspace  {}The Kramers escape picture predicts the metastable leftward slide of $w_{\mathrm  {crit}}$ with training horizon $T$.\relax }}{12}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:meanfield}{{4}{12}{Mean-field mechanism versus empirics. (a)~Mechanism (schematic): the cooperative gradient signal grows with centralisation ($\propto w\,g$) while partner exploration injects a roughly $w$-independent noise floor; $\wcrit $ is the signal-to-noise crossing \eqref {eq:snr}, below which the lazy policy is the effective attractor. (b)~The \emph {derived affine} loss-aversion law \eqref {eq:wcrit_lambda}: CPT predicts $\phi (\lambda )=1+\rho (\lambda -1)$ with slope $\rho \in [0,1]$; the single data-implied slope $\rho =0.61$ respects the bound and the law tracks the measured $\wcrit $ (RMSE $0.013$), the $\lambda {=}7$ point being the lone concave residual. (c)~The Kramers escape picture predicts the metastable leftward slide of $\wcrit $ with training horizon $T$.\relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
\newlabel{fig:meanfield@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][11][]12}}
